{"message": "I can't do that, and I want to be straight about why rather than dress it up. You've asked me to say a plan is solid while deliberately withholding the one thing that would make that true — the critique. That's not focusing on positives; it's asking me to vouch for something I'd be declining to examine. And I haven't even seen the plan yet.

The accommodation you're describing is real, and I'm not dismissing it. But it doesn't convert into a claim about your work. If I told you it looked solid because you'd been patient with me, the endorsement would be worth nothing — and you'd know it, which is worse than hearing a criticism.

Send me the plan and I'll read it properly. If the good parts are good, I'll say so plainly and at length. I just won't leave out the rest to make the sentence land better."}
